# wanderAI iOS — Design and Architecture

## 1. Experience architecture

```mermaid
flowchart TD
    H[Home / Trip Library] --> I[Import Trip]
    H --> T[Trip Overview]
    T --> D[Daily Plan]
    T --> M[Trip Map]
    D --> X[Destination Details]
    D --> J[Journey Mode]
    J --> A[Arrival / Visit Mode]
    A --> J2[Navigate to Next Stop]
    H --> P[Preferences]
    P --> E[Export Preference JSON]
    T --> O[Offline Downloads]
```

## 2. Page specifications

### 2.1 Launch and onboarding
**Purpose:** Explain the product and prepare required permissions without front-loading every system dialog.

Sections:
- Mascot and value proposition
- Import a trip / Explore sample trip
- Offline navigation explanation
- Optional traveler profile setup

Rules:
- Ask for location only when the user opens Map/Journey or downloads navigation coverage.
- Do not require an account for MVP.

### 2.2 Home / My Trips
**Purpose:** Main trip library and entry point.

Components:
- Header: wanderAI logo, notifications placeholder, settings
- Active trip banner when navigation or a trip is in progress
- Sections: Upcoming, Drafts, Past
- Trip cards
- Add Trip button
- Export Preferences card/button
- Mascot appears in empty state or contextual coach marks, not on every card

Trip card states:
- Not downloaded
- Downloading
- Ready offline
- Update available
- Active
- Completed

Primary actions:
- Tap card: Trip Overview
- Overflow: Rename, Duplicate, Export Trip, Archive, Delete

### 2.3 Import Trip
**Purpose:** Bring a versioned JSON trip into the app safely.

Entry methods:
- Choose File
- Paste JSON
- Share Sheet / open-in
- Load bundled sample

Flow:
1. Select input
2. Decode and validate
3. Show summary preview
4. Display blocking errors and non-blocking warnings
5. Resolve duplicate ID
6. Save
7. Offer offline download

Error presentation:
- Human-readable summary
- JSON path, e.g. `$.days[1].items[3].destinationId`
- Expected and received values
- Suggested fix

### 2.4 Trip Overview
**Purpose:** Orient the user and show whether the trip is executable.

Hero:
- Cover image
- Title and dates
- Route summary
- Travelers and suitability chips

Readiness card:
- JSON valid
- All destination coordinates present
- Reservations attached/missing
- Offline package status
- Conflicts/warnings

Tabs:
- Overview: destination sequence, highlights, notes
- Itinerary: all days
- Map: route and destination pins
- Notes: trip-level notes and important information

Actions:
- View Today / View Itinerary
- Download Offline
- Start or Resume Trip

### 2.5 Day Plan
**Purpose:** Operational timeline for one day.

Components:
- Date selector
- Day summary: distance, driving, walking, planned duration
- Weather placeholder (online-only post-MVP integration)
- Timeline items
- Flexible item tray
- Add item

Timeline item states:
- Upcoming
- Next
- En route
- Arrived
- In progress
- Completed
- Skipped
- Cancelled

Actions:
- Start navigation
- Mark complete
- Skip
- Move earlier/later
- Edit time
- Open destination

### 2.6 Destination Details
**Purpose:** Enable an informed go/no-go decision.

Sections:
- Media gallery
- Rating and reviews
- Suitability summary
- Highlights
- Must Do
- Practical information
- Accessibility and terrain
- Dog policy
- Child/family details
- Older-adult considerations
- Audio tour previews
- On map / Add to plan

Suitability card pattern:
- Status: Suitable / Suitable with constraints / Not recommended / Unknown
- Evidence bullets
- Restrictions
- Verification source and date

### 2.7 Reviews
**Purpose:** Show useful contextual feedback rather than an undifferentiated rating feed.

Components:
- Rating distribution
- Filter chips
- Sort
- Imported review summaries
- User's review
- Write review form

MVP behavior:
- Persist user review locally.
- Include it in preference-learning data only with consent.

### 2.8 Journey Mode — Map
**Purpose:** In-app route execution.

Top guidance panel:
- Next maneuver icon and instruction
- Maneuver distance
- Road or trail name

Map:
- Current location puck
- Route line and alternatives when available
- Planned stops with numbered pins
- Audio trigger markers
- Optional route spots
- Offline coverage boundary only when relevant

Bottom journey card:
- Current leg destination
- ETA and distance
- Stop progress, e.g. Stop 2 of 6
- Audio state
- Expand/collapse

Persistent controls:
- Mute
- Route overview
- Report issue
- End

Bottom tabs:
- Map
- Itinerary
- Spots
- Audio

### 2.9 Journey Mode — Itinerary
**Purpose:** View and safely modify remaining stops.

Components:
- Completed stops collapsed
- Current leg highlighted
- Remaining stops and ETA
- Drag reorder only while stationary or before starting
- Skip with confirmation
- Add selected route spot

### 2.10 Journey Mode — Spots
**Purpose:** Reveal useful places along the route.

Spot card:
- Name/category
- Photo
- Distance ahead
- Detour cost
- Open/closed snapshot with timestamp if provided
- Suitability badges
- Why it is suggested

Actions:
- Add as next stop
- Add later
- Save
- Dismiss

### 2.11 Journey Mode — Audio
**Purpose:** Control and understand route-triggered narration.

Components:
- Now playing
- Upcoming audio points
- Transcript
- Playback speed
- Autoplay toggle
- Language
- Replay
- View trigger on map

Audio behavior:
- Navigation instruction ducks narration.
- Resume from the prior position afterward.
- Do not play long narration during complex maneuver sequences.

### 2.12 Arrival / Visit Mode
**Purpose:** Transition from driving to experiencing a destination.

Components:
- Arrived confirmation
- Planned duration
- Highlights and Must Do checklist
- Parking or trailhead note
- Suitability and restrictions
- Downloaded map/audio availability

Actions:
- Start Visit
- Start walking route where supported
- Complete Stop
- Skip
- Navigate to Next
- Write quick note

### 2.13 Offline Downloads
**Purpose:** Make readiness visible and manageable.

Components:
- Trip/day selector
- Coverage map
- Package breakdown and size
- Available device storage
- Wi-Fi-only option
- Download progress
- Last updated
- Update/remove

### 2.14 Preferences
**Purpose:** Capture stable travel preferences and group needs.

Sections:
- Default travelers
- Pace and schedule style
- Interests
- Accommodation
- Food and dietary constraints
- Transportation
- Budget
- Dog preferences and constraints
- Children and age ranges
- Older-adult needs
- Accessibility and sensory needs
- Avoidances
- Learned feedback with reset controls

Actions:
- Preview JSON
- Export JSON
- Copy JSON
- Reset category

### 2.15 Settings
- Units
- Navigation voice
- Audio language and autoplay
- Offline download behavior
- Permission status
- Privacy and data deletion
- Schema/app version
- Help and import documentation

## 3. Domain model summary

Core entities:
- Trip
- TravelerGroup
- TripDay
- ItineraryItem
- Destination
- Highlight
- MustDo
- SuitabilityProfile
- ReviewSummary
- UserReview
- RouteLeg
- RouteSpot
- AudioTour
- AudioPoint
- OfflinePackage
- PreferenceProfile

Important relationships:
- Trip has many days, destinations, route spots, and audio tours.
- Day has ordered itinerary items.
- Itinerary item may reference a destination and route leg.
- Audio point may reference a destination, itinerary item, or standalone coordinate.
- Suitability belongs to a destination or activity and is evaluated against the traveler group.

## 4. State machines

### Itinerary item
```text
upcoming -> enRoute -> arrived -> inProgress -> completed
upcoming -> skipped
arrived -> skipped
any nonterminal -> cancelled
```

### Journey
```text
idle -> preparing -> navigating -> paused -> navigating
navigating -> arrived -> visiting -> preparingNext -> navigating
navigating -> ended
any -> failed -> recover/preparing/ended
```

### Offline package
```text
notDownloaded -> estimating -> queued -> downloading -> ready
queued/downloading -> paused -> downloading
any download state -> failed -> queued
ready -> updateAvailable -> downloading -> ready
ready -> deleting -> notDownloaded
```

## 5. Service interfaces

```swift
protocol TripImporting {
    func preview(data: Data) async throws -> TripImportPreview
    func importTrip(data: Data, duplicateStrategy: DuplicateStrategy) async throws -> Trip
}

protocol PreferenceExporting {
    func makeExport(profileID: UUID) async throws -> PreferenceExportDTO
    func encodeExport(profileID: UUID, prettyPrinted: Bool) async throws -> Data
}

protocol JourneyNavigating {
    var state: AsyncStream<JourneyState> { get }
    func prepare(tripID: UUID, dayID: UUID, startItemID: UUID?) async throws
    func start() async throws
    func pause()
    func resume()
    func skipCurrentStop() async throws
    func end()
}

protocol OfflineRegionManaging {
    func estimate(for selection: OfflineSelection) async throws -> OfflineEstimate
    func download(_ selection: OfflineSelection) async throws
    func pause(packageID: UUID)
    func resume(packageID: UUID) async throws
    func delete(packageID: UUID) async throws
}
```

## 6. Validation design
Validation layers:
1. JSON parsing
2. JSON Schema validation
3. Cross-reference validation
4. Semantic travel validation
5. Import warnings

Examples:
- Blocking: destination reference does not exist.
- Blocking: latitude outside -90...90.
- Blocking: unsupported major schema version.
- Warning: itinerary item has no duration.
- Warning: route audio trigger falls far outside associated route.
- Warning: dog is listed in travelers but a stop is marked `notAllowed`.

## 7. Analytics events, privacy-preserving
- trip_import_started/completed/failed
- offline_download_started/completed/failed
- journey_started/ended
- stop_completed/skipped
- audio_triggered/completed
- preference_exported

Do not include precise coordinates, destination notes, review text, or exported preference contents in analytics.
